WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.780
Film Courage: If someone comes to you and
says “Hey I have this great script that

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:03.780
I wrote.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.450
How do I get a studio to make it?

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.800
Adam, please…I know you work in Hollywood
or you know people in Hollywood, can I get

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.370
this script to you?”

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:17.630
Writer/Director/Actor, Adam Cushman: What
do I say?

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:19.910
I mean it depends, it’s situational.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:20.910
I mean it depends.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.050
It depends on the conversation, what type
of movie it is and everything else.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:34.329
I read a lot but beyond that if you’re asking
me when filmmakers come to me and they’re

00:00:34.329 --> 00:00:35.720
like “How do I get this made?”

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.390
Film Courage: “I need Hollywood to make
it.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.570
If they see it, it’s going to be the next
(fill in the blank).”

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:49.879
Adam: This almost always comes with them attached
to play the lead.

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:57.140
In which case…since that is most cases I
usually say I think you should consider finding

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.610
a way to go make your film and make it on
your terms.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:13.560
It’s just not 1978 anymore…I mean I wish
it was but it’s not and the days of sitting

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:19.930
in a coffee shop chewing your nails waiting
on a deal is not realistic.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.439
It’s not going to happen that way.

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:29.060
So I would say try if you can get any traction
that way, terrific.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:34.740
But look at what’s out there and if that
person is saying I want a studio to make my

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.270
film for 40-50 million dollars, I want to
be in theaters say look what is in theaters

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.020
though.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.709
Is there anything like what you’re doing
right now?

00:01:44.709 --> 00:01:50.279
And that’s not to discourage people from
making the film but I would say find a way

00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:54.159
to go make it your way because they are going
to try and take it away from you anyway and

00:01:54.159 --> 00:02:01.759
they’re going to have weird ideas and take
it in their own direction but that’s my

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:02.759
advice.

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:07.640
Like I think we’re just living in an era
where you just have to go do it and it’s

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:08.640
easier than ever.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.390
I mean look at John Cassavetes and look at
all the great…he’s the bar.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:19.989
He is what we alway refer to as the person
who went out and did it and he shot on film

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:23.110
and they had lab fees and we don’t have
that.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:30.010
I mean we can have that but we also have the
Alexas and the Red cameras and this technology

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.900
where we can go shoot now.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.209
So there is just no excuse.

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:36.329
Film Courage: What if they say “I am not
in Hollywood.

00:02:36.329 --> 00:02:41.689
I’m in a state that there is no industry
here?”

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:42.689
Adam: Dude, even better.

00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:45.170
Everyone’s going to want to help you.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:50.500
I mean seriously everyone is going to want
to be…not everyone but people are going

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.989
to want to help in the community because they
want to help you succeed.

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:56.489
They’ve never seen a film done before.

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:00.069
It’s interesting, it’s novel.

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:03.400
I found it much easier to shoot outside of
Los Angeles.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:09.049
You know, here I went to a burger place (it’s
the one next to Paramount Pictures because

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:10.049
I had a scene at the place).

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:16.511
And I went in the morning and I talked to
the guy and he was “The Guy” and I’m

00:03:16.511 --> 00:03:22.280
like “Bare bones, indie film, I would need
like three hours, I would come during off

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.580
hours, no problem.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.739
Even night is fine, I can fake it for the
day.”

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.170
He’s like “Yeah, $20,000.”

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.530
I’m like “Copy that.”

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.010
It’s where you are.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:37.950
There are people who will pay that and that
is a thing so I think if you’re living outside

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:43.689
of LA, it’s a benefit and you can get a
lot more mileage out of whatever you’re

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:44.689
doing.”

00:03:44.689 --> 00:03:48.143
Film Courage: And you shot RESTRAINT [one
of Adam’s films] where?

00:03:48.143 --> 00:03:49.540
Adam: Predominantly in Apple Valley.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.079
Film Courage: Is that San Bernardino County
[California]?

00:03:52.079 --> 00:03:53.730
Adam: I think so.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:54.980
It’s high desert.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.900
And that was like that too.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.050
We found that to be the case.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:06.439
Our producer was from there so we had certain
resources but that not withstanding it was

00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:14.560
a very inexpensive permit and they were very
open to us shooting throughout the city and

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.540
different areas, gas stations, cleaners, parks,
things like that.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.090
And they were just very helpful.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:24.100
Film Courage: Who did you contact to get that
permit?

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.120
Did they have a film office?

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.729
Adam: Yes, the producer did that but I think
we got it out of necessity.

00:04:29.729 --> 00:04:39.901
We were shooting at a house and then someone
from the city stopped by and was like “Do

00:04:39.901 --> 00:04:40.949
you guys have a permit?”

00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:42.430
I’m like “Do we need it for the house?”

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:43.430
And he’s like “Yes.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.950
You can keep going, don’t worry about it
but you need to get that.”

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.120
I was like Wow!

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.610
Here they would shut you down and take your
memory card and make an example out of you.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:58.120
But you go one hour outside of LA and they’re
just ultra helpful.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.120
Question For The Viewers: What advice do you
have for someone who wants Hollywood to make

00:04:59.120 --> 00:04:59.122
their movie?

